{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q003_actin_like_atpase_divergence","answer":"SCOPe places these catalytic domains in separate families—Actin/HSP70, hexokinase, and glycerol kinase—within the Actin-like ATPase superfamily (c.55.1). This superfamily belongs to the ribonuclease H-like motif fold (c.55) in the α/β protein class.

The superfamily assignment supports remote homology: their conserved nucleotide-binding catalytic cores are inferred to descend from a common ancestor despite substantial sequence and functional divergence. This is a stronger evolutionary claim than merely sharing a fold; fold or class membership alone does not establish common ancestry.

It does not justify transferring specific functions between families. Actin participates in cytoskeletal assembly, HSP70 in protein folding, hexokinase phosphorylates hexoses, and glycerol kinase phosphorylates glycerol. Shared ancestry does not make all of them equivalent ATP-hydrolyzing enzymes or establish identical substrates, regulation, or interaction partners. A cross-family structure may provide a template for the conserved core, but substrate-binding regions, insertions, domain movements, and interfaces require independent validation. The classification applies to the domains, not automatically to the complete proteins; one representative cannot capture the functional diversity of the entire superfamily.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
